sections.s2_technical:
The generated SQL for `findByIdIn(List<Long> ids)` with 10,000 IDs looks like this:

```sql
SELECT * FROM trades WHERE trade_id IN (?, ?, ?, ?, ... /* 10,000 question marks */ ?)
```

This causes five specific problems. First, the SQL string itself is enormous — potentially hundreds of kilobytes. Parsing this takes measurable time. Second, every different count of parameters is a DIFFERENT prepared statement: IN with 500 params and IN with 501 params are cached separately. With varying list sizes, the plan cache gets thousands of entries that are never reused. Third, Oracle has a hard limit of 1000 bind parameters in an IN clause. PostgreSQL has no hard limit but performance degrades significantly above a few thousand. Fourth, the query optimizer may not use the index efficiently for 10K probes — it might choose a sequential scan instead. Fifth, transmitting 10K IDs over the network adds latency.

```java
// (Avoid) BAD: one query with 10K IDs
List<Trade> trades = tradeRepo.findByIdIn(allTenThousandIds);
// Generates: SELECT ... WHERE trade_id IN (?, ?, ?, ... × 10000)
// Oracle: ORA-01795: maximum number of expressions in a list is 1000!
// PostgreSQL: works but plan cache destroyed, slow execution

// (Recommended) FIX 1: Chunk into batches of 500
List<Trade> allTrades = new ArrayList<>();
List<List<Long>> chunks = Lists.partition(allTenThousandIds, 500);
for (List<Long> chunk : chunks) {
    allTrades.addAll(tradeRepo.findByIdIn(chunk));
}
// 20 queries × 500 IDs each. Each query is fast. Plan cached and reused.

// (Recommended) FIX 2: Server-side filter (best when possible)
// Instead of: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE id IN (10000 IDs)
// Use:        SELECT * FROM trades WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND trade_date = '2024-01-15'
// The DB filters server-side — no ID shipping needed!

// (Recommended) FIX 3: Temp table / native query with VALUES
@Query(nativeQuery = true, value = """
    SELECT t.* FROM trades t
    JOIN (VALUES (?1), (?2), (?3)) AS ids(id) ON t.trade_id = ids.id
    """)
List<Trade> findByIdsJoinValues(List<Long> ids);
```

Spring Data JPA's `findByIdIn` uses Hibernate's IN clause directly. For production code with potentially large ID lists, always add chunking logic in the service layer:

```java
@Service
public class TradeService {
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 500;

    public List<Trade> findByIds(List<Long> ids) {
        if (ids.size() <= CHUNK_SIZE) {
            return tradeRepo.findAllById(ids);  // small list — single query OK
        }
        // Large list — chunk it
        return Lists.partition(ids, CHUNK_SIZE).stream()
            .flatMap(chunk -> tradeRepo.findAllById(chunk).stream())
            .toList();
    }
}
```

